1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,960
In any war, the key to victory is mobility.

2
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:05,440
The victors are the ones who can move.

3
00:00:05,740 --> 00:00:10,509
In 1984, a new era in military transportation was heralded by the U.S.

4
00:00:10,510 --> 00:00:11,940
Army's high-mobility,

5
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:15,360
multi-purpose vehicle, better known as the Humvee.

6
00:00:15,660 --> 00:00:18,220
But this big rig is really the end of the story.

7
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:22,360
A story that begins with a vehicle that captured hearts around the world

8
00:00:22,361 --> 00:00:26,020
and caused an even bigger stir in its day, the Jeep.

9
00:00:26,021 --> 00:00:32,400
By the end of World War II, nearly every guy in America knew about the Jeep and knew he wanted one.

10
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:35,580
It just became an overnight sensation and a legend.

11
00:00:36,140 --> 00:00:42,040
Every one of these G.I.s that came in as a young kid in the Army, 17, 18 years old, an entire generation said,

12
00:00:42,440 --> 00:00:44,460
Look at this thing. I just love it.

13
00:00:44,760 --> 00:00:50,400
Perhaps the most American of all vehicles, this is the story about the evolution of the Jeep.

14
00:00:51,020 --> 00:00:54,640
Now, Big Rigs of Combat, Jeeps on Modern Marvels.

15
00:00:56,020 --> 00:01:10,020
This odd machine transformed the very nature of war

16
00:01:10,280 --> 00:01:13,920
by replacing the cornerstone of ancient warfare.

17
00:01:16,060 --> 00:01:17,140
The horse.

18
00:01:17,141 --> 00:01:30,520
for 4 000 years of recorded history the horse was the primary vehicle that the army used to

19
00:01:30,521 --> 00:01:38,060
carry equipment transport troops it was good off-road terrain vehicle it was the fastest

20
00:01:38,061 --> 00:01:44,360
thing alive at that point for warfare so it performed splendidly for 4 000 years

21
00:01:44,361 --> 00:01:56,640
but horses required grooming exercise nourishment and training

22
00:01:56,641 --> 00:02:04,540
and like the soldiers they served horses were often casualties of war

23
00:02:04,541 --> 00:02:13,680
in world war one a dark innovation in combat would revolutionize mobility

24
00:02:13,681 --> 00:02:19,720
mechanized warfare.

25
00:02:20,980 --> 00:02:23,760
The bloody battlegrounds of the Great War

26
00:02:23,761 --> 00:02:26,680
were the proving grounds for dozens of new machines.

27
00:02:27,960 --> 00:02:29,960
From the Ford Model T

28
00:02:29,961 --> 00:02:32,820
to the Mark I tank.

29
00:02:34,520 --> 00:02:36,600
When 19th century strategy

30
00:02:36,601 --> 00:02:39,040
confronted these 20th century weapons,

31
00:02:39,041 --> 00:02:41,240
the result was catastrophic.

32
00:02:41,241 --> 00:02:46,700
We deployed horse cavalry to Europe in World War I.

33
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:52,860
The theory was that we would somehow, with infantry, punch a hole through the German defenses,

34
00:02:53,180 --> 00:02:55,920
and in comes the cavalry, and they would go back and swing their sabers

35
00:02:55,921 --> 00:03:00,360
and do whatever this cavalry does when it gets in the enemy's rear, and it didn't work that way.

36
00:03:01,160 --> 00:03:05,900
Although primitive, these new machines often became effective replacements for the horse.

37
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:10,680
The horse could not keep up with the motor vehicle.

38
00:03:11,240 --> 00:03:13,660
They would not tire, they would not acquire food.

39
00:03:14,540 --> 00:03:18,900
But the machines of the First World War had one fatal flaw.

40
00:03:20,580 --> 00:03:26,480
Trucks and tanks started taking over, the personnel carrying, the munitions carrying.

41
00:03:26,481 --> 00:03:31,040
And now the only thing that the horse could do that all of this other equipment could not do

42
00:03:31,041 --> 00:03:33,660
was effectively go cross-country in the mud.

43
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:39,260
By war's end, no military had solved this dilemma.

44
00:03:40,120 --> 00:03:47,180
But military strategists were convinced that a highly mobile vehicle would be instrumental to future victories.

45
00:03:48,060 --> 00:03:53,300
After the war, they all set out to invent the world's first mechanical horse.

46
00:03:56,640 --> 00:03:57,340
The U.S.

47
00:03:57,341 --> 00:04:03,180
military made several attempts to build such a machine in the 1920s and 1930s.

48
00:04:03,181 --> 00:04:03,881
30s.

49
00:04:08,660 --> 00:04:12,040
One design in particular generated high hopes.

50
00:04:13,340 --> 00:04:16,760
The snake in the grass vehicle was a very low profile vehicle.

51
00:04:17,120 --> 00:04:22,980
It resembled in many ways like a kid's go-kart that two grown men could lay down on in a

52
00:04:22,981 --> 00:04:24,740
prone position, face forward.

53
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:28,560
One was the driver, the other one had a .30 caliber machine gun mounted.

54
00:04:29,020 --> 00:04:32,540
So this thing was so low that it could just run through the grass and you couldn't see

55
00:04:32,541 --> 00:04:33,241
it.

56
00:04:33,242 --> 00:04:40,900
But the vehicle's low clearance made it impossible to traverse rough terrain and proved impractical for the battlefield.

57
00:04:42,260 --> 00:04:46,940
The snake in the grass acquired the less than flattering nickname, belly flopper.

58
00:04:49,140 --> 00:04:53,704
Despite numerous efforts, by the late 1930s, the horse remained the U.S.

59
00:04:53,705 --> 00:04:57,380
Army's most reliable mode of cross-country transportation.

60
00:05:03,180 --> 00:05:08,280
Time was about to run out.

61
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:15,380
September 1st, 1939, the face of war is changed as the Nazi forces slash into Poland.

62
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:23,200
By 1940, Adolf Hitler's forces had swept through Poland, Belgium and France,

63
00:05:23,880 --> 00:05:26,560
conquering land faster than any army in history.

64
00:05:26,561 --> 00:05:33,560
Germany, defeated in World War I, understood the importance of mechanization.

65
00:05:33,561 --> 00:05:38,560
And by World War II, they perfected it.

66
00:05:38,561 --> 00:05:45,560
The Germans were really the only ones to seize on the singular opportunity

67
00:05:45,561 --> 00:05:48,560
that the tanks and armored forces represented.

68
00:05:48,561 --> 00:05:53,560
The Blitzkrieg, or lightning strike strategy,

69
00:05:53,561 --> 00:05:56,540
was the culmination of years of research and preparation

70
00:05:56,541 --> 00:06:00,560
and built upon a highly mobile fleet of machines.

71
00:06:07,100 --> 00:06:10,580
Elite panzers and Stuka bombers sliced through Europe,

72
00:06:11,100 --> 00:06:14,340
reinforced by the swift and revolutionary reconnaissance car,

73
00:06:15,080 --> 00:06:15,880
the Kubelwagen.

74
00:06:17,340 --> 00:06:19,020
The German Kubelwagen,

75
00:06:19,480 --> 00:06:22,960
which essentially means bucket car in German or bucket wagon,

76
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:27,580
came out of the development of the 1930s Volkswagen.

77
00:06:28,440 --> 00:06:33,040
They produced this very open, buckety, ugly-looking type of a body

78
00:06:33,041 --> 00:06:36,740
that went on top of the regular Volkswagen chassis.

79
00:06:37,740 --> 00:06:40,660
Its innovative design and impressive speed

80
00:06:40,661 --> 00:06:45,520
made the Kubelwagen one of the most effective vehicles in Germany's arsenal.

81
00:06:53,560 --> 00:06:58,700
As Adolf Hitler's armored divisions took continental Europe by storm,

82
00:06:59,320 --> 00:07:03,640
the United States was forced to reckon with its own shortcomings in armored development,

83
00:07:04,440 --> 00:07:10,860
including a shocking deficiency of tanks and still no replacement for the horse.

84
00:07:12,440 --> 00:07:17,780
All through the 1930s, the government was really working on this concept of replacing the horse.

85
00:07:17,781 --> 00:07:23,720
And because of the Blitzkrieg, it became very important that we put something into place to do that

86
00:07:23,721 --> 00:07:29,000
because we just could not stand up against the German army on horseback like we were at the time.

87
00:07:29,160 --> 00:07:32,720
You're dealing with relatively ancient technology here

88
00:07:32,721 --> 00:07:37,500
and with technology that we knew was pretty fragile on the battlefield, and we needed to do better.

89
00:07:38,340 --> 00:07:39,970
In the coming months, the U.S.

90
00:07:39,971 --> 00:07:43,120
Army desperately attempted to fill the gap in its arsenal.

91
00:07:44,040 --> 00:07:46,880
The solution would come from the unlikeliest of places.

92
00:07:46,881 --> 00:07:51,960
A private company with only 15 employees on the verge of bankruptcy.

93
00:07:55,180 --> 00:07:59,960
One of the first peacetime uses for the Jeep was as a replacement for the farm tractor.

94
00:08:00,600 --> 00:08:02,480
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

95
00:08:07,300 --> 00:08:10,680
As the war ravaged Europe in the summer of 1940,

96
00:08:11,200 --> 00:08:15,720
the U.S. Army finally drew up specifications for a new reconnaissance vehicle.

97
00:08:15,721 --> 00:08:18,640
able to compete with Germany's Kubelwagen.

98
00:08:20,820 --> 00:08:25,140
The list described a machine unlike anything ever built.

99
00:08:25,960 --> 00:08:28,500
The government was initially looking for a vehicle

100
00:08:28,501 --> 00:08:31,180
that didn't have any more than a 75-inch wheelbase

101
00:08:31,181 --> 00:08:33,660
and wasn't more than 36 inches tall.

102
00:08:35,140 --> 00:08:37,940
It also had to carry a 500-pound payload,

103
00:08:38,440 --> 00:08:41,600
yet the weight of the vehicle could be no more than 1,000 pounds.

104
00:08:41,601 --> 00:08:50,560
the most daunting specification of all however was the army's deadline they wanted this done in 49

105
00:08:50,561 --> 00:08:56,140
days that's taking a vehicle in concept and producing a prototype and have it delivered

106
00:08:56,141 --> 00:09:00,280
in 49 days and if you didn't get it there on time you were going to be fined per day by the government

107
00:09:00,281 --> 00:09:08,320
the army frantically requested bids from 135 american civilian auto and truck manufacturers

108
00:09:08,321 --> 00:09:14,300
The daunting deadline and specifications scared away all but two.

109
00:09:15,460 --> 00:09:18,400
The successful Willys Overland in Toledo, Ohio

110
00:09:18,401 --> 00:09:24,440
and the tiny American Bantam Car Company of Butler, Pennsylvania.

111
00:09:25,820 --> 00:09:27,760
Though the odds were against them,

112
00:09:28,680 --> 00:09:31,520
American Bantam was the lone company to make the cut.

113
00:09:32,820 --> 00:09:35,240
It was an uphill battle from the beginning.

114
00:09:35,241 --> 00:09:41,800
The president of American Bantam Company decided that he wanted to go after this bid.

115
00:09:42,380 --> 00:09:49,820
However, American Bantam Car Company in Butler, Pennsylvania, only had approximately 15 employees at the time, none of which were engineers.

116
00:09:50,900 --> 00:09:56,940
Looking to fill the void, American Bantam courted Carl Probst, a talented engineer from Detroit.

117
00:09:57,660 --> 00:10:04,680
They approached Probst and said, Carl, we've got 49 days, produce a vehicle from nothing, and we have no money.

118
00:10:05,240 --> 00:10:09,780
Well, Probst turned around, and his first several reactions were, you guys are nuts.

119
00:10:10,980 --> 00:10:14,460
Lured by the challenge, Probst accepted Bantam's offer.

120
00:10:16,240 --> 00:10:23,320
On Wednesday, July 17, 1940, Probst and the head of the Bantam team, Harold Crist, set to work.

121
00:10:24,340 --> 00:10:28,100
The effort continued around the clock for the next five weeks.

122
00:10:28,860 --> 00:10:33,880
The only way I could describe what they were doing is I would call ruthless engineering.

123
00:10:33,881 --> 00:10:38,180
If they didn't have it on the shelf and they couldn't find it easily,

124
00:10:38,340 --> 00:10:41,160
they would send people out to forge and pick up whatever they had,

125
00:10:41,420 --> 00:10:43,360
bring it back into the shop and modify it.

126
00:10:43,580 --> 00:10:46,180
These guys were motivated and they were smart people.

127
00:10:49,880 --> 00:10:51,960
Fenders were fabricated by hand.

128
00:10:52,840 --> 00:10:54,620
Hoods were dragged from junkyards.

129
00:10:55,820 --> 00:10:59,280
Other vital components were cannibalized from older Bantam vehicles.

130
00:10:59,281 --> 00:11:06,100
so september first rolled around and these guys are looking at each other with stuff all over the

131
00:11:06,101 --> 00:11:10,200
floor the pieces aren't together and they're just saying we aren't going to be able to do it it's

132
00:11:10,201 --> 00:11:19,260
impossible the deadline 5 p.m september 23rd 1940 by the night of the 22nd the team had accomplished

133
00:11:19,261 --> 00:11:26,020
all they could they called their prototype the brc the bantam reconnaissance car

134
00:11:26,021 --> 00:11:32,640
next morning they mounted up they drove it at 25 miles an hour and a totally untested vehicle just

135
00:11:32,641 --> 00:11:39,780
to break break the engine in with only half an hour to spare the bantam team delivered their

136
00:11:39,781 --> 00:11:47,520
new invention to camp holliver the army's testing ground in maryland no one had ever seen anything

137
00:11:47,521 --> 00:11:52,820
like it boy look at that you know this this is state-of-the-art technology and the first thing

138
00:11:52,821 --> 00:11:56,020
they did was they hopped in and just kind of preliminarily drove around and everybody was

139
00:11:56,021 --> 00:12:01,024
like all excited about it. The bigger question was, would it work?

140
00:12:01,025 --> 00:12:02,920
To find out, they put six

141
00:12:02,921 --> 00:12:06,021
additional prototypes through grueling tests.

142
00:12:06,022 --> 00:12:09,260
They basically beat the living daylights out of

143
00:12:09,261 --> 00:12:10,251
the thing.

144
00:12:10,252 --> 00:12:17,980
They just went into the woods, they went up and down hills, they loaded it up,

145
00:12:18,300 --> 00:12:22,800
they put people in it, they tried everything that they could think of to see what this thing

146
00:12:22,801 --> 00:12:28,040
could do and they were just totally flabbergasted because it did so much more than they expected it

147
00:12:28,041 --> 00:12:28,741
to do.

148
00:12:28,742 --> 00:12:35,280
Equipped with four-wheel drive, a new invention at the time, the BRC easily climbed

149
00:12:35,281 --> 00:12:38,747
and descended the steepest grades.

150
00:12:38,748 --> 00:12:44,660
The powerful engine, a Continental Y4112, gave the BRC the

151
00:12:44,661 --> 00:12:49,024
unprecedented ability to tow nearly half of its own weight.

152
00:12:49,025 --> 00:12:51,760
The Bantam design was simple, rugged,

153
00:12:51,761 --> 00:12:53,980
and an astounding success.

154
00:12:56,040 --> 00:12:58,800
One of the first officers to drive it predicted,

155
00:12:59,440 --> 00:13:01,240
this unit will make history.

156
00:13:03,040 --> 00:13:05,200
Bantam's persistence had paid off.

157
00:13:06,340 --> 00:13:07,900
This is part of the American character,

158
00:13:08,020 --> 00:13:11,220
the seat of the pants ability to take a concept

159
00:13:11,221 --> 00:13:14,040
and deal with it and bring it to fruition.

160
00:13:14,620 --> 00:13:17,420
And other nations couldn't do that,

161
00:13:17,480 --> 00:13:19,100
and I think it's part of the American spirit.

162
00:13:19,101 --> 00:13:24,160
It's a can-do feeling, a can-do spirit.

163
00:13:25,480 --> 00:13:31,220
Unfortunately for Bantam, turning in the winning car did not win them the prized production contract.

164
00:13:32,100 --> 00:13:37,520
The Quartermaster Corps was saying, we have a war to win, and we're not worried about what's fair necessarily,

165
00:13:37,740 --> 00:13:43,160
but we need the biggest guy, the best guy, to get this on time and delivered in a quality fashion,

166
00:13:43,500 --> 00:13:45,160
and therefore they went with the larger companies.

167
00:13:45,161 --> 00:13:51,380
With the threat of imminent war, the Army gave the final production contracts

168
00:13:51,381 --> 00:13:54,300
