00:18:36,887 --> 00:18:40,940
The jeep's ingeniously simple design was one of

230
00:18:40,941 --> 00:18:42,342
its greatest assets.

231
00:18:42,343 --> 00:18:47,880
Because it was so adaptable, resourceful GIs and engineers immediately invented

232
00:18:47,881 --> 00:18:52,640
new uses for it. Some ideas were a little too far ahead of their time.

233
00:18:54,600 --> 00:18:59,220
When soldiers in Asia and Europe discovered that the Jeep was hampered by deep water,

234
00:19:00,160 --> 00:19:04,860
they improvised several techniques that enabled the Jeep to cross rivers and streams.

235
00:19:06,240 --> 00:19:08,505
None of them were very simple.

236
00:19:08,506 --> 00:19:12,960
The Ford Motor Company constructed a waterproof hull around

237
00:19:12,961 --> 00:19:18,600
the Jeep's exterior and called the experiment the General Purpose Amphibious Vehicle, or

238
00:19:18,601 --> 00:19:26,900
GPAV. But it proved ultimately unusable on the battlefield. While the GPAV swam like

239
00:19:26,901 --> 00:19:32,260
a sea lion, the additional 1,000 pounds of waterproofing materials slowed the vehicle

240
00:19:32,261 --> 00:19:33,084
to a crawl.

241
00:19:33,085 --> 00:19:39,300
Another little-known and equally curious Jeep innovation was called the Roto-Buggy.

242
00:19:42,960 --> 00:19:46,960
Equipping the Jeep with rotors and an aerodynamic tail,

243
00:19:46,961 --> 00:19:51,960
Army engineers designed the roto buggy to fly itself to remote locations.

244
00:19:52,960 --> 00:19:56,960
But the flying Jeep never really made it off the ground.

245
00:19:58,960 --> 00:20:02,960
Jeep alterations made in the field were usually more successful.

246
00:20:03,960 --> 00:20:08,960
Nearly 1,000 miles from the European front, in the deserts of North Africa,

247
00:20:08,961 --> 00:20:13,600
North Africa, the jeep was modified to play one of its most crucial roles.

248
00:20:16,020 --> 00:20:22,480
By the spring of 1942, Germany's infamous General Erwin Rommel had invaded Egypt and

249
00:20:22,481 --> 00:20:25,120
was rapidly sweeping eastward across North Africa.

250
00:20:27,740 --> 00:20:32,580
In response, the Allied Air Force relentlessly bombed German targets.

251
00:20:33,320 --> 00:20:37,580
But an air campaign alone was not enough to stop Germany's powerful army.

252
00:20:38,960 --> 00:20:42,980
their solution would become the stuff of legend, the SAS.

253
00:20:47,300 --> 00:20:50,960
Glamorized by the 1960s television series Rat Patrol,

254
00:20:51,560 --> 00:20:53,840
the SAS, or Special Air Services,

255
00:20:54,340 --> 00:20:56,320
was an elite division of the British Army

256
00:20:56,321 --> 00:21:00,260
that mounted swift clandestine assaults on German targets in Africa.

257
00:21:02,620 --> 00:21:06,740
Their weapon of choice was the most reliable vehicle in their arsenal.

258
00:21:08,960 --> 00:21:10,640
The U.S. Army Jeep.

259
00:21:12,320 --> 00:21:18,160
It was camouflaged the color of sand and rigged with extra gas and water cans,

260
00:21:18,460 --> 00:21:24,460
which allowed the SAS to operate behind enemy lines for unprecedented periods of time.

261
00:21:27,880 --> 00:21:32,440
But the most significant SAS innovation involved weaponry.

262
00:21:33,740 --> 00:21:37,700
For the first time, the Jeep was used as an attack vehicle.

263
00:21:38,960 --> 00:21:43,760
Equipped with .50 caliber Brownings and twin Vickers machine guns,

264
00:21:43,761 --> 00:21:48,960
the SAS Jeeps had the deadly capability of firing thousands of rounds per minute.

265
00:21:50,160 --> 00:21:54,440
There's several stories of them coming into air bases and just running down the airfields

266
00:21:54,441 --> 00:21:57,160
and just blowing up every plane they could see in sight and run back out

267
00:21:57,161 --> 00:22:02,660
and come out with no casualties because they were so lightning quick and adept at desert warfare.

268
00:22:03,280 --> 00:22:08,100
The SAS's unrelenting raids eventually helped defeat the Germans on the African front.

269
00:22:08,101 --> 00:22:16,460
This enabled the Allies to advance, setting the stage for the invasion of Normandy, where the jeep would once again play a crucial role.

270
00:22:17,940 --> 00:22:21,540
This is the day for which free people long have waited.

271
00:22:24,460 --> 00:22:26,880
This is D-Day.

272
00:22:31,160 --> 00:22:33,460
June 6, 1944.

273
00:22:33,461 --> 00:22:39,420
the Allies invaded Normandy, France in an all-out attempt to defeat Germany.

274
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:44,140
After two months of brutal fighting,

275
00:22:44,580 --> 00:22:47,740
the Allies gained a stronghold on the French and Belgian coast.

276
00:22:52,980 --> 00:22:56,260
In their pursuit of the rapidly retreating German army,

277
00:22:56,261 --> 00:23:02,140
thousands of U.S. Sherman tanks consumed more than 800,000 gallons of fuel daily.

278
00:23:03,460 --> 00:23:10,460
Supplying the rapidly moving armor proved nearly impossible

279
00:23:10,461 --> 00:23:13,460
because trains in the region had been destroyed.

280
00:23:13,461 --> 00:23:18,460
There was a great deal of gasoline

281
00:23:18,461 --> 00:23:21,460
and supplies in the Normandy area.

282
00:23:21,461 --> 00:23:24,460
There was more than enough to supply all the armies.

283
00:23:24,461 --> 00:23:28,460
Normandy was just bristling with depots of all types.

284
00:23:28,461 --> 00:23:32,460
Gasoline by the thousands of gallons.

285
00:23:32,461 --> 00:23:36,180
But they couldn't get it to the front fast enough.

286
00:23:36,840 --> 00:23:38,440
Enter the jeep.

287
00:23:39,620 --> 00:23:43,160
The Army initially outfitted jeeps with locomotive wheels

288
00:23:43,161 --> 00:23:47,580
and used them to transport supplies via rail lines.

289
00:23:48,820 --> 00:23:51,320
When this failed to keep up with the demand,

290
00:23:51,720 --> 00:23:57,100
the Allied commanders devised an ingenious transportation line called the Red Ball Express.

291
00:23:57,101 --> 00:24:02,920
To red ball something was to FedEx it or to send it priority mail.

292
00:24:03,400 --> 00:24:07,780
When you said you wanted something red balled, you wanted to get it there as quickly as possible.

293
00:24:07,920 --> 00:24:09,560
And of course that was a term that the Army used.

294
00:24:10,480 --> 00:24:14,600
The Red Ball Express utilized nearly every jeep and truck in the area.

295
00:24:15,860 --> 00:24:21,000
The Red Ball was established August 25th, 1944.

296
00:24:22,200 --> 00:24:25,460
It was seat of the pants, completely ad lib.

297
00:24:25,461 --> 00:24:30,760
They just started grabbing trucks, and within 36 hours, the first trucks were on the road.

298
00:24:32,400 --> 00:24:37,180
By the fourth day, some 6,000 trucks and jeeps began a one-way convoy,

299
00:24:37,640 --> 00:24:40,860
looping from the Cherbourg Peninsula to Paris and back.

300
00:24:42,320 --> 00:24:47,020
Manned mostly by African Americans, the Red Ball became an unstoppable force.

301
00:24:47,500 --> 00:24:49,900
The jeep was crucial to the Red Ball's success.

302
00:24:51,180 --> 00:24:54,680
The jeeps would ride herd and make sure that no one straggled.

303
00:24:54,681 --> 00:25:02,640
You'd have lead Jeeps and rear Jeeps, NCOs in the Jeeps or officers, and they were kind of like the sheepdogs.

304
00:25:03,760 --> 00:25:09,200
If you hadn't had the Red Ball, I think it's safe to say that the war in Europe would have taken longer to win.

305
00:25:10,280 --> 00:25:13,840
You wouldn't have supplied these troops, and the armies would have come to a halt.

306
00:25:13,841 --> 00:25:23,700
The jeep had begun its service in World War II

307
00:25:23,701 --> 00:25:26,460
as a simple solution to a daunting problem

308
00:25:26,461 --> 00:25:29,380
replacing the function of the horse

309
00:25:29,381 --> 00:25:37,000
But by the war's end

310
00:25:37,001 --> 00:25:38,960
due to its unfailing service

311
00:25:38,961 --> 00:25:42,480
the jeep had become the embodiment of the war effort

312
00:25:42,481 --> 00:25:56,481
from europe to africa to asia the allies reliance on the jeep to transport troops and supplies

313
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:08,000
proved crucial in the defeat of the axis powers it was the american ability to harness its

314
00:26:08,001 --> 00:26:15,200
industrial power and put it into machines, whether it be a jeep or a truck, was what

315
00:26:15,201 --> 00:26:17,600
won the war for the Americans, for the Allies.

316
00:26:17,800 --> 00:26:21,840
There's no doubt that the jeep definitely had a major effect on the war.

317
00:26:21,960 --> 00:26:28,200
I think Eisenhower had at one time quoted that there were three major things that had

318
00:26:28,201 --> 00:26:32,180
really saved us in the war and allowed us to do what we did, and one of the three was

319
00:26:32,181 --> 00:26:32,881
the jeep.

320
00:26:32,882 --> 00:26:40,420
I have no doubt in my mind that those 49 days, if those guys hadn't accomplished what they did, we would not be sitting here in the same world today.

321
00:26:41,420 --> 00:26:44,680
But the Jeep's service would not end with World War II.

322
00:26:47,760 --> 00:26:50,860
Its most challenging days were yet to come.

323
00:26:50,861 --> 00:26:56,780
It is believed that the origin of the name Jeep

324
00:26:56,781 --> 00:26:59,220
comes from the little animal Eugene the Jeep,

325
00:26:59,221 --> 00:27:03,580
who first appeared in the Popeye comic strip in September 1937.

326
00:27:04,360 --> 00:27:06,260
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

327
00:27:10,260 --> 00:27:10,960
1945.

328
00:27:12,180 --> 00:27:13,420
The war was over.

329
00:27:14,340 --> 00:27:15,640
The victors were home.

330
00:27:17,520 --> 00:27:20,420
And the Jeep began its life as a civilian.

331
00:27:20,860 --> 00:27:25,745
The Jeep became a very popular vehicle. There were articles written about it.

332
00:27:25,746 --> 00:27:28,980
It just became an overnight sensation and a legend.

333
00:27:30,300 --> 00:27:37,798
Every one of these GIs that came in as a young kid in the Army, 17, 18 years old, an entire generation said, look at this thing.

334
00:27:37,799 --> 00:27:39,380
I just love it. It's great.

335
00:27:39,381 --> 00:27:41,173
They became so attached to them.

336
00:27:41,174 --> 00:27:45,320
It was almost as if they owned a horse and it was part of their equipment.

337
00:27:45,321 --> 00:27:46,021
it.

338
00:27:46,022 --> 00:27:50,800
As the war was coming to a close after D-Day, everybody, Willys, Ford, everybody was thinking

339
00:27:50,801 --> 00:27:54,240
about, let's take this thing and capitalize, because when these guys all get out, there's a

340
00:27:54,241 --> 00:27:55,853
market for them to buy it.

341
00:27:55,854 --> 00:28:00,320
Starting in 1944, Willys Overland started doing testing on civilian-type

342
00:28:00,321 --> 00:28:01,021
Jeeps.

343
00:28:01,022 --> 00:28:10,920
Willys Overland, one of the Jeep's original manufacturers, created the convertible Jeepster

344
00:28:10,921 --> 00:28:16,060
and the wagon, billed as the ultimate family vehicles.

345
00:28:18,780 --> 00:28:22,680
Even the U.S. Postal Service was eager to buy the Jeep.

346
00:28:24,560 --> 00:28:27,680
Between 1944 and 1947,

347
00:28:28,400 --> 00:28:34,220
Willys Overland sales skyrocketed from $4 million to $138 million.

348
00:28:34,221 --> 00:28:39,300
It was the birthplace of today's recreational market,

349
00:28:39,301 --> 00:28:42,560
of the SUVs that you see running around.

350
00:28:42,880 --> 00:28:44,360
Before the concept of the Jeep,

351
00:28:44,440 --> 00:28:46,860
there was no such thing in that size of a vehicle

352
00:28:46,861 --> 00:28:48,280
or anything close to that size.

353
00:28:48,540 --> 00:28:50,700
So you can thank what you're driving around in today

354
00:28:50,701 --> 00:28:54,000
to essentially going back to those 49 days at the Butler plant.

355
00:28:55,620 --> 00:28:59,380
The Jeep's astronomical success in the civilian market, however,

356
00:28:59,381 --> 00:29:03,560
did not overshadow its crucial ongoing role in the military.

357
00:29:03,561 --> 00:29:12,960
In 1951, American troops were called to defend South Korea from the aggressions of the Communist North.

358
00:29:15,360 --> 00:29:21,100
Only months after their arrival, the simmering conflict erupted into a full-fledged war.

359
00:29:22,760 --> 00:29:25,460
Once again, the jeep was sent to the battlefield.

360
00:29:26,400 --> 00:29:31,300
The standard World War II jeep saw service right up through Korea.

361
00:29:31,301 --> 00:29:36,700
There were many, many World War II vehicles and jeeps used because we had so many of them left over.

362
00:29:37,160 --> 00:29:41,880
However, there were some flaws that the government perceived that they wanted fixed.

363
00:29:42,580 --> 00:29:49,600
In the Korean War, the wounded were transported to Army hospitals mainly in jeeps as they had been in World War II.

364
00:29:50,900 --> 00:29:57,080
But the litters rigged to carry the men were unstable and ill-equipped for emergency medical care.

365
00:30:01,300 --> 00:30:09,720
Willys Overland introduced the M-170, designed to carry a medical crew to care for wounded soldiers while in transit.

366
00:30:14,940 --> 00:30:20,680
During the Korean War, Army payloads dramatically increased in both size and weight.

367
00:30:22,000 --> 00:30:24,880
It was getting tougher for the little jeep to keep up.

368
00:30:24,881 --> 00:30:33,040
To keep pace with these changes in military technology, the M38 model Jeep was introduced in 1950.

369
00:30:34,260 --> 00:30:37,160
The M38 itself almost looks like a World War II Jeep.

370
00:30:37,700 --> 00:30:39,888
It has flat hood and flat fenders.

371
00:30:39,889 --> 00:30:45,100
It has a waterproof fording system in it, different things that were added to it.

372
00:30:46,320 --> 00:30:54,100
Its most important improvement, a six-cylinder hurricane engine, enabled the M38 to carry larger payloads.

373
00:30:54,880 --> 00:30:58,440
But these modifications presented new problems.

374
00:30:59,440 --> 00:31:01,300
It had a little bigger engine in it.

375
00:31:01,780 --> 00:31:03,220
It had a higher center of gravity.

376
00:31:04,040 --> 00:31:05,840
And it turned over more easily.

377
00:31:06,180 --> 00:31:11,000
I can remember a famous maneuver in the wintertime in Germany when the roads were frozen.

378
00:31:11,001 --> 00:31:12,700
And I wrecked seven.

379
00:31:13,260 --> 00:31:15,420
My driver and I wrecked seven of those vehicles.

380
00:31:15,580 --> 00:31:18,360
Not because he was not a good driver or I not a good rider,

381
00:31:18,540 --> 00:31:20,460
but it was just that the thing was terribly unstable.

382
00:31:20,461 --> 00:31:32,100
A decade later, in the midst of the Vietnam War, the Ford Motor Company introduced the M151, hoping to eliminate the weaknesses of the M38.

383
00:31:32,101 --> 00:31:41,400
with the m151 jeep they were trying to have that vehicle replace several of the earlier models or

384
00:31:41,401 --> 00:31:45,900
versions of the jeep and do it all or they wanted to become a reconnaissance vehicle they wanted to

385
00:31:45,901 --> 00:31:54,100
add kits become an ambulance they want it to be a do-all vehicle almost in a modular format the

386
00:31:54,101 --> 00:32:00,700
The most notable modification of the M151 was in its suspension.

387
00:32:00,701 --> 00:32:06,860
The M151 series vehicles incorporated an independent suspension which was relatively revolutionary

388
00:32:06,861 --> 00:32:08,300
for the time.

389
00:32:08,301 --> 00:32:12,240
The difference between a regular suspension as used on the early Jeeps which had solid

390
00:32:12,241 --> 00:32:17,040
axles and the independent suspension on the M151s is that this independent suspension

391
00:32:17,041 --> 00:32:21,980
allowed all four wheels to be on the ground over any type of terrain, therefore increasing

392
00:32:21,981 --> 00:32:22,980
traction.

393
00:32:22,981 --> 00:32:34,020
Instead of alleviating the Jeep's hazardous rollovers, however, the M151's newly designed suspension only compounded the problem.

394
00:32:34,440 --> 00:32:42,340
If the vehicle ever lifts up off the ground because of this independent suspension, what happens with each one of the wheels is they tuck in like this.

395
00:32:42,540 --> 00:32:46,280
So when they hit the ground, those wheels can collapse and the vehicle can roll over.

396
00:32:46,620 --> 00:32:52,480
Where with a solid axle Jeep, if you come off the ground, they're stuck to a solid axle, so they come right back down in the same width tread.

397
00:32:52,980 --> 00:32:58,980
Here in slow motion is an M151A1 being driven over a ramp at 15 miles per hour.

398
00:32:59,540 --> 00:33:01,200
Note the sharp steer to the left.

399
00:33:02,020 --> 00:33:04,260
This is at 15 miles an hour.

400
00:33:04,760 --> 00:33:12,680
So as a result, the M151 series had a tremendous number of accidents and fatalities with GIs and even some civilian owners

401
00:33:12,681 --> 00:33:18,100
because of the fact that if they drove it improperly and it came up off the ground, they would roll and kill themselves.

402
00:33:18,101 --> 00:33:24,420
Modifications such as the addition of a roll bar

403
00:33:24,421 --> 00:33:26,160
failed to solve the problem

404
00:33:26,161 --> 00:33:35,660
Tests conducted in the use of roll bars, seat belts and shoulder harnesses

405
00:33:35,661 --> 00:33:40,540
reveal that safety cannot be guaranteed in a turnover situation

406
00:33:48,100 --> 00:33:53,087
By the end of the Vietnam War in 1973, the U.S.

407
00:33:53,088 --> 00:33:58,180
Army Jeep had been in service for over 30 years.

408
00:34:00,240 --> 00:34:06,100
Militaries from South America to Southeast Asia had found it indispensable.

409
00:34:08,840 --> 00:34:17,920
And despite the numerous modifications, all Jeeps remained virtually identical to the dimensions and weight of the original Bantam prototype,

410
00:34:18,100 --> 00:34:25,681
of 1941. But in the 1980s, the heyday of the Jeep would come to a close.

411
00:34:25,682 --> 00:34:27,260
Advancements in

412
00:34:27,261 --> 00:34:32,420
military weaponry and tactics would bring new demands upon battlefield vehicles and

413
00:34:32,421 --> 00:34:36,780
give way to a new breed of military transportation.

414
00:34:36,781 --> 00:34:42,221
The first U.S. trademark was obtained in 1950.

415
00:34:42,222 --> 00:34:47,780
Today, Daimler Chrysler owns 1,100 registration

416
00:34:47,781 --> 00:34:53,100
for the jeep trademarks throughout the world modern marvels will return in a moment

417
00:34:53,101 --> 00:35:03,300
in the years following the vietnam war the weaponry and vehicles of the u.s arsenal became

418
00:35:03,301 --> 00:35:10,080
increasingly high-tech but while the military was becoming ever more sophisticated overall

419
00:35:10,081 --> 00:35:16,780
its workhorse the jeep was still using 1940s technology they were first off getting very old

420
00:35:16,781 --> 00:35:18,260
and as they say, long and tooth.

421
00:35:18,780 --> 00:35:21,020
The industry, the automotive industry,

422
00:35:21,100 --> 00:35:23,180
was able to do a lot more things with newer materials,

423
00:35:23,300 --> 00:35:24,920
including composites and plastics,

424
00:35:24,921 --> 00:35:27,260
which they weren't able to do back in the 40s and 50s

425
00:35:27,261 --> 00:35:28,540
when they built and designed the Jeep.

426
00:35:30,140 --> 00:35:32,060
By the late 1970s,

427
00:35:32,360 --> 00:35:36,460
the U.S. Army recognized that the Jeep's military future was over.

428
00:35:37,900 --> 00:35:41,720
After more than 30 years of service, the Jeep was retired.

429
00:35:43,820 --> 00:35:45,460
With the demise of the Jeep,

430
00:35:45,461 --> 00:35:49,980
the Army was forced to address the crucial problem of mobility once again.

431
00:35:51,520 --> 00:35:53,420
In order to do that, you need to take a look at the battlefield

432
00:35:53,421 --> 00:35:55,880
and decide what it's going to look like in the future battlefield,

433
00:35:56,220 --> 00:35:57,540
because that's where the thing's going to fight.

434
00:35:58,760 --> 00:36:02,040
How far do you want it to be able to drive without refueling?

435
00:36:02,400 --> 00:36:04,040
What kind of protection do you have to have?

436
00:36:04,080 --> 00:36:05,180
That means what is the threat?

437
00:36:06,740 --> 00:36:09,200
So you really begin with an evaluation of the threat.

438
00:36:10,340 --> 00:36:13,880
The Army envisioned a new jack-of-all-trades vehicle

439
00:36:13,881 --> 00:36:16,800
that could carry more passengers and equipment than the jeep,

440
00:36:17,100 --> 00:36:20,420
tow larger and heavier loads, and achieve higher speeds.

441
00:36:23,160 --> 00:36:27,920
The behemoth that was delivered to the military was designed by A.M. General,

442
00:36:28,260 --> 00:36:30,720
a distant relative of the Willys-Overland Company.

443
00:36:31,520 --> 00:36:37,220
It was called the High Mobility Multipurpose Wheeled Vehicle, or Humvee.

444
00:37:01,900 --> 00:37:07,060
It was two feet wider and more than three feet longer than any Jeep.

445
00:37:07,680 --> 00:37:12,640
Despite its massive size, it was designed to meet crucial Army specifications.

446
00:37:12,641 --> 00:37:17,240
Most of the wars we've fought in the past century have occurred in the European theater.

447
00:37:17,241 --> 00:37:22,140
144 inches is the maximum width allowable in European tunnels for trains.

448
00:37:22,141 --> 00:37:24,140
That's as far wide as you can get.

449
00:37:24,141 --> 00:37:27,640
So no vehicle made for the United States military is wider than that.

450
00:37:27,641 --> 00:37:31,640
The Humvee sat twice as high off the ground as the Jeep

451
00:37:31,641 --> 00:37:36,640
and could easily clear obstacles that would stop any other vehicle in its tracks.

452
00:37:36,641 --> 00:37:41,640
Up here is where the clearance numbers are measured, and that's 16 inches from the ground.

453
00:37:41,641 --> 00:37:48,980
The standard off-wheel vehicle today, Fords, Chevys, and so forth, are anywhere from 7 to 8 inches in clearance.

454
00:37:49,280 --> 00:37:50,000
So this is double.

455
00:37:51,400 --> 00:37:57,560
And obviously the wider stance or the wider vehicle also allows better balance in very high-angle terrain.

456
00:37:58,540 --> 00:38:03,180
Powered by a V8 diesel engine with a 25-gallon fuel capacity,

457
00:38:03,460 --> 00:38:07,480
the Humvee had more than double the horsepower of the most sophisticated Jeep.

458
00:38:08,160 --> 00:38:11,740
He had even had the remarkable ability to drive underwater.

459
00:38:12,660 --> 00:38:14,200
It was designed not to float.

460
00:38:14,560 --> 00:38:17,540
It is specifically designed to sink immediately.

461
00:38:18,000 --> 00:38:19,200
And it's done for a reason.

462
00:38:19,460 --> 00:38:22,020
They don't want you to lose the tread on the ground.

463
00:38:22,520 --> 00:38:27,120
So as you hit water, most vehicles will float for a while before they go down and then start to be able to drive.

464
00:38:27,240 --> 00:38:31,320
This vehicle can go through any level of water, any level of lake, any level of anything,

465
00:38:31,700 --> 00:38:34,380
as long as water does not get in through the air filter for your diesel.

466
00:38:35,100 --> 00:38:37,160
They put this at the high point of the vehicle.

467
00:38:37,161 --> 00:38:42,420
Now, in the military, they also have a periscope, so they're able to raise this up, and they also raise up the mufflers.

468
00:38:42,800 --> 00:38:49,400
This will go into a lake or a pond nonstop and just continue along the bottom, literally, until you run out of gas or your breath.

469
00:38:50,300 --> 00:38:55,400
The Humvee also performs other tricks that enable it to overcome the most difficult terrain.

470
00:38:56,200 --> 00:39:04,300
From the cab, on the move, at any time, you can inflate and deflate the tire pressure for anywhere from 0 to 50 pounds per square inch.

471
00:39:04,960 --> 00:39:09,080
And what that really does is expand the tire pattern or tread, the footprint,

472
00:39:09,460 --> 00:39:13,000
and allows you much more contact with the road for less slippage.

473
00:39:14,300 --> 00:39:17,840
The Humvee was subjected to months of brutal Army testing.

474
00:39:19,420 --> 00:39:22,040
But its toughest challenge was yet to come.

475
00:39:23,220 --> 00:39:29,940
In 1990, the Humvee was dispatched on its first call to duty, the Persian Gulf War.

476
00:39:29,941 --> 00:39:36,720
When a massive three-week air assault failed to dislodge Saddam Hussein from Kuwait,

477
00:39:37,340 --> 00:39:44,300
the U.S.-led coalition began a ground war led by a formidable array of mechanized fighting machines.

478
00:39:46,120 --> 00:39:49,800
Leading the modern cavalry charge into Kuwait was the Humvee.

479
00:39:51,080 --> 00:39:53,680
How do you feel about leading the charge in a Humvee?

480
00:39:53,960 --> 00:39:54,840
I think it's great.

481
00:39:56,340 --> 00:39:59,780
These vehicles went out there untested in a combat environment.

482
00:39:59,940 --> 00:40:04,720
untested in such large quantities, if this broke down and you couldn't get parts, you had a problem.

483
00:40:05,340 --> 00:40:08,920
So it was really, as they say, a lot of nail-biting going on at that point when these were deployed.

484
00:40:11,520 --> 00:40:16,120
On the first night of the attack, there was a Marine Corps group that was out front

485
00:40:16,121 --> 00:40:20,320
that was guarding a supply, forward supply area, with fuel and so forth.

486
00:40:20,540 --> 00:40:24,140
And the Iraqis had an attack going, and there was fog and so forth.

487
00:40:24,141 --> 00:40:28,460
And the only equipment they had besides their personal weapons, their M-16s,

488
00:40:28,461 --> 00:40:31,340
were Humvees with a couple of M60 machine guns on top.

489
00:40:31,720 --> 00:40:33,940
And they used these to repel the attack long enough

490
00:40:33,941 --> 00:40:36,780
until the Air Force could come and use fighter aircraft

491
00:40:36,781 --> 00:40:38,960
and bombers to knock back the Iraqi attack.

492
00:40:39,080 --> 00:40:41,940
So these vehicles held it off to keep that resupply depot

493
00:40:41,941 --> 00:40:44,600
available for helicopters and other vehicles that came through.

494
00:40:46,220 --> 00:40:48,500
In the unforgiving Arabian desert,

495
00:40:48,860 --> 00:40:52,960
over 20,000 Humvees exceeded the Army's highest expectations.

496
00:40:53,860 --> 00:40:58,160
It's a statement by an Iraqi brigade commander

497
00:40:58,161 --> 00:41:00,460
who said something to this effect,

498
00:41:00,560 --> 00:41:04,480
that the United States Air Force bombed us for three weeks

499
00:41:04,481 --> 00:41:07,060
and we lost a few tanks and some people.

500
00:41:08,020 --> 00:41:10,360
And then along came the 2nd Cavalry

501
00:41:10,361 --> 00:41:13,420
and we lost the whole brigade in an afternoon.

502
00:41:15,460 --> 00:41:18,060
On the battleground and behind the lines,

503
00:41:18,061 --> 00:41:20,100
the Humvee played many roles,

504
00:41:20,420 --> 00:41:24,840
from missile launcher to ambulance.

505
00:41:24,841 --> 00:41:31,820
the new vehicle even withstood unusual testing from the soldiers of desert storm

506
00:41:31,821 --> 00:41:37,700
the game they used to play during the gulf war was to see who could get the vehicle stuck

507
00:41:37,701 --> 00:41:42,020
in the bogs in the mud they were never able to do it they were that good

508
00:41:42,021 --> 00:41:48,880
as the jeep before it the humvee's performance and popularity on the battlefield

509
00:41:48,881 --> 00:41:51,520
aroused curiosity on the home front.

510
00:41:52,440 --> 00:41:55,020
The initial public reaction when this vehicle came out

511
00:41:55,021 --> 00:41:57,280
was more like they were laughing at it.

512
00:41:57,420 --> 00:41:59,480
Who would own this thing? This is ridiculous.

513
00:42:00,180 --> 00:42:03,760
But when Arnold Schwarzenegger expressed interest in purchasing a Humvee,

514
00:42:04,060 --> 00:42:06,660
the radical new vehicle caught on with the public.

515
00:42:07,300 --> 00:42:12,100
His civilian Humvee, or Hummer, was aptly nicknamed the Terminator.

516
00:42:12,560 --> 00:42:15,360
He actually went to the factory to see the whole assembly line,

517
00:42:15,361 --> 00:42:18,560
and the first civilian one that came off the line, he bought.

518
00:42:18,880 --> 00:42:21,060
And as I understand it today, he has five of them.

519
00:42:26,500 --> 00:42:35,860
World War II correspondent Ernie Pyle called the Jeep faithful as a dog, strong as a mule, agile as a goat.

520
00:42:37,160 --> 00:42:45,040
When asked how the Allies won the war, Eisenhower, Patton, and Churchill all listed the Jeep among the most crucial factors.

521
00:42:45,041 --> 00:42:50,520
I think the spirit of the Jeep still lives in the concept and the idea of the Humvee.

522
00:42:50,940 --> 00:42:55,360
It's still the idea to provide a full-use vehicle for off-road capability.

523
00:42:57,860 --> 00:42:58,820
I miss the Jeep.

524
00:42:58,821 --> 00:43:03,620
It's still here in the form of the Wrangler, but I don't think it's gone either.

525
00:43:03,621 --> 00:43:04,460
It'll be back.

526
00:43:05,280 --> 00:43:12,760
From the horse, to the Jeep, to the Humvee, success in battle has always depended on mobility.

527
00:43:15,040 --> 00:43:24,000
As long as men continue to fight wars, victory will belong to those who can move.

